In JPA (Java Persistence API), implementing filtering for search is a very common interview topic.
 There are multiple ways depending on how dynamic your search is.

Let’s go from simple → advanced 👇

✅ 1. Simple Filtering using Method Naming (Spring Data JPA)

If filters are fixed, use method names:

List<User> findByName(String name);
List<User> findByAgeGreaterThan(int age);
List<User> findByNameAndCity(String name, String city);

✔ Easy
❌ Not scalable for dynamic filters

✅ 2. Using @Query (JPQL)

For more control:

@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.name = :name AND u.city = :city")
List<User> findUsers(@Param("name") String name,
                     @Param("city") String city);

✔ Better control
❌ Still static

✅ 3. Dynamic Filtering using Criteria API (IMPORTANT 🔥)

Best when filters are optional (search screen).

public List<User> searchUsers(String name, Integer age) {
    CriteriaBuilder cb = entityManager.getCriteriaBuilder();
    CriteriaQuery<User> cq = cb.createQuery(User.class);
    Root<User> root = cq.from(User.class);

    List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();

    if (name != null) {
        predicates.add(cb.like(root.get("name"), "%" + name + "%"));
    }

    if (age != null) {
        predicates.add(cb.equal(root.get("age"), age));
    }

    cq.where(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0]));

    return entityManager.createQuery(cq).getResultList();
}

✔ Fully dynamic
✔ Used in real-world search APIs
❌ Verbose

✅ 4. Spring Data JPA Specification (MOST IMPORTANT ⭐)

This is the best approach for interviews + real projects.

👉 Step 1: Extend repository

public interface UserRepository
    extends JpaRepository<User, Long>, JpaSpecificationExecutor<User> {
}

👉 Step 2: Create Specification

public class UserSpecification {

    public static Specification<User> hasName(String name) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            name == null ? null : cb.like(root.get("name"), "%" + name + "%");
    }

    public static Specification<User> hasAge(Integer age) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            age == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("age"), age);
    }
}

👉 Step 3: Use it

Specification<User> spec = Specification
        .where(UserSpecification.hasName(name))
        .and(UserSpecification.hasAge(age));

List<User> users = userRepository.findAll(spec);

✔ Clean
✔ Reusable
✔ Dynamic filtering
✔ Industry standard

✅ 5. Using Example (QBE - Query by Example)
User user = new User();
user.setName("Ali");

Example<User> example = Example.of(user);

List<User> users = userRepository.findAll(example);

✔ Simple
❌ Limited (no complex queries like range, joins)

🔥 Interview Summary (Must Say)

If interviewer asks:

👉 Best approach?

Use Spring Data JPA Specification for dynamic filtering.

👉 Why?

Handles optional filters
Clean and reusable
Supports complex queries
💡 Real-world Example (API)
GET /users?name=ali&age=25

→ Convert params into Specification
→ Pass to repository
→ Return filtered results